WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.769
A coffee purchase moves 30 from wallet w-17 to the cafe.

2
00:00:06.769 --> 00:00:12.655
The debit is on one database server, wallets, and the credit on another, payouts.

3
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:14.692
Both must happen, or neither.

4
00:00:14.692 --> 00:00:20.975
If wallets commits first and payouts then crashes, the customer has paid and the cafe got nothing.

5
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:27.711
Two-phase commit adds a coordinator: a process that runs the transaction and decides how it ends.

6
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.304
Phase one is prepare.

7
00:00:29.304 --> 00:00:34.249
The coordinator sends each server its changes, then asks if it can commit.

8
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:40.184
Wallets checks its rules, and writes the changes to its log on disk, marked prepared.

9
00:00:40.184 --> 00:00:41.401
Then it votes yes.

10
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.236
That yes is a promise.

11
00:00:43.236 --> 00:00:48.440
Wallets can't abort on its own anymore, and it keeps its lock on w-17.

12
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:51.511
Payouts does the same, and votes yes too.

13
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:56.351
Every vote is yes, so the coordinator writes commit to its own log on disk.

14
00:00:56.351 --> 00:00:59.491
That line is the moment the whole transfer commits.

15
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:03.310
Phase two: the coordinator tells both servers to commit.

16
00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.717
Each one commits, and drops its lock.

17
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:07.115
Now run it again.

18
00:01:07.115 --> 00:01:13.648
Both servers vote yes, the coordinator logs commit, and then it crashes before telling anyone.

19
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.721
Payouts has promised to commit if told to, and nobody is telling it anything.

20
00:01:18.721 --> 00:01:19.616
It's in doubt.

21
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:23.138
It can't commit: maybe the wallets server voted no.

22
00:01:23.138 --> 00:01:29.230
It can't abort either: maybe the coordinator logged commit, and wallets has already committed.

23
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.008
So it waits.

24
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.933
While it waits, it holds its locks, so every purchase at that cafe queues.

25
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:41.264
Restarting the database doesn't help: the prepared transaction comes back from disk, locks and all.

26
00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:44.259
Only the coordinator's log knows the outcome.

27
00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:49.160
When it comes back, it reads the log and finishes every transfer left in doubt.

28
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:53.818
Every server promises, one log line decides, then everyone commits.

29
00:01:53.818 --> 00:01:57.432
Lose the coordinator in between, and the servers wait.
